teacher Luis Abreu is showing us around  a suburb in the north of Rio de Janeiro  he wants us to see what the local people  find extremely frustrating  a resident takes us to an abandoned  construction site this was supposed to  be a new school they stopped building  four years ago meanwhile the children  are being schooled in substandard  temporary classrooms they have to walk  through the favela which is dangerous  those who aren't finishing this building  are destroying the hopes and future of  our children the school ruin in South  schwow is symbolic  Brazil's infrastructure is getting  increasingly dilapidated the government  is doing little outside the upscale  neighborhoods  no one's investing anything here is  there really any public investment I  don't think so we walk through the  streets here and see that nothing is  being done  no one is investing any money we feel  betrayed the feeling of betrayal may  soon be getting worse the new finance  minister show Hakeem lavey has promised  to do everything he can to slash the  deficit and reduce inflation he's hoping  that will make Brazil more attractive to  investors that means cutting government  expenditure left and right critics like  economics professor victor aruz show  fear that will cause a snowball effect  when there's no investment no orders a  place that means less production and  fewer jobs our population is growing  each year we need more jobs and a  growing economy what's being planned  will destroy jobs with the country's  crumbling infrastructure and sputtering  economy at all show things the planned  austerity program will plunge  once-booming Brazil into a recession the  alternative would be the exact opposite  the government has to launch large-scale  investment plans that would create jobs  construction materials like sand and  concrete would be coming from local  businesses Luiz Abreu doesn't care where  the concrete might come from for the  school in South schwow  he just like construction to start again  he says everything is getting worse and  he doesn't believe the government's  forecast of economic growth in 2015
 

